Stop expr's substr from truncating a long length.
The length was passed to xstrndup as a size_t, so where size_t is
narrower than the 64-bit integer, as on 32-bit systems, its high bits
were dropped: "expr substr abcdef 2 4294967297" printed "b" instead of
"bcdef", and a length of 4294967296 printed nothing. Now a length longer
than the string is limited to the string's length first.

---
 coreutils/expr.c |    3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

--- a/coreutils/expr.c
+++ b/coreutils/expr.c
@@ -418,6 +418,9 @@
 		 || i1->u.i <= 0 || i2->u.i <= 0)
 			v = str_value("");
 		else {
+			/* Don't let a long length wrap around in size_t */
+			if (i2->u.i > (arith_t) strlen(l->u.s))
+				i2->u.i = strlen(l->u.s);
 			v = xmalloc(sizeof(VALUE));
 			v->type = STRING;
 			v->u.s = xstrndup(l->u.s + i1->u.i - 1, i2->u.i);
